Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-11-2001||10:46 AM||||I decided to wire my headlights up direct again, <P>Anyone know of a good toggle type of switch that will work for the headlights? (one way for up,one for down then the middle for dead)?<BR>Thanks<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Jake_Dragon||08-11-2001||12:14 PM||||I want to do this as well. What will stop the motor when it is in the full open and closed position? I just fixed my gears and don't want to strip them again.<BR>I also want to move the new relays to the sealed part of the front compartment.<BR>I don't have the trunk switch so I was looking for something small that would fit there.||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-11-2001||12:18 PM||||You need the toggle switch to stop it when it raises/lowers, right now I have mine hooked up to wires, and can cross them to raise/lower them.<P>think I'll hit the auto supply store after work and see if I can locate a usable switch..<P>If I find one, and it works good, I'll get you a photo and the part number.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-11-2001||01:33 PM||||This is a pretty bad idea for the motors. You would be better to surf the salvage yards for a latter Fiero or other GM with the Gen 2 motors. The same Gen 2 motor system apeared on some of the sunbirds with the little half doors over the lghts and several other GM cars. (GM likes to reuse parts as much as they can across car lines. Much cheaper that way.)<P>There's no easy reliable and safe way to simply stuff a switch in there. If you don't figure out a way to make use of the torque limit switches you'll wreck the motors in fairly short order.<P>Looking briefly at the Gen 1 schematic, you can only eliminate one of the 3 relays with a manual switch. The other 2 work with the limit switches to kill power at the proper moment to not trash the motor or gearing.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mickey_Moose||08-11-2001||01:40 PM||||would it not just be easier to replace the relay(s) or switch? (whatever the original problem was?)<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000005||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-11-2001||02:01 PM||||mines in rough shape.. missing relays,cut wires, etc.. I tried another relay..no power through it.. no power through ANY of the wires up at the main relay etc..<P>just fed up with it.. <P>99.9% of the time my headlights stay up anyways..  just mostly want them working by a switch incase I ever need to lower them for any reason..<P>(saves doing them manually)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadaver||08-11-2001||04:57 PM||||Get a manual and fix them right or get another wiring harness. they are cheap. I installed a switch that left mine up so at night I didnt have to keep running them up and down. they worked just like stock with tehe switch on the when off they went up with the headlight switch and stayed up when turned off. But know I have the flush lights so its all moot anyway,||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jake_Dragon||08-11-2001||05:04 PM||||I had a little run in with a tree and pinched the wires up front. I planed on using most of the stock parts.<BR>I want the separate switch so I can turn off the lights without lowering them.<BR>I also want to move most if not all of the relays and wiring into the sealed part of the front compartment.<BR>Right now I am fighting with an EGR and could care less if the lights work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> ||||reg||1
