Z||000010||cadaver||08-11-2001||11:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jake_Dragon:<BR><B>I had a little run in with a tree and pinched the wires up front. I planed on using most of the stock parts.<BR>I want the separate switch so I can turn off the lights without lowering them.<BR>I also want to move most if not all of the relays and wiring into the sealed part of the front compartment.<BR>Right now I am fighting with an EGR and could care less if the lights work   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thats what I did. All you do is put a switch inline of the white wire from the headlight switch. I used a rocker and put it in the panel under the steering wheel.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||862M4inCA||08-13-2001||10:55 AM||||Well, there are a couple of leads coming directly off the headlight switch on the dash that operates ONLY the up or down depending on if your lights are on or off. My suggestion is to get a simple toggle switch to mount somewhere that breaks the connection to make them go down. That way your lights (if in the down position) will go up when you turn them on, but will not go down until that switch is closed to allow the relay to get the signal that the lights are off. Definately would save wear on the motors if you do a lot of stop and go night time driving.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Carrolles||08-13-2001||11:17 AM||||Cadaver is right.  Put a switch inline in the white wire coming off the back of the light switch.  When the switch is open the lights will raise and go on normally but will stay up when the lights are turned off.  When closed everything is back to normal.<P>This is great for flashing your headlights or (for those of you in the frozen north) for leaving them up when the lights are turned off so they don't freeze shut.||||reg||1
